    'title' => 'The global rise of Nollywood',
    'teaser' => "Nollywood has grown from a resourceful home-video industry into one of the world's most energetic centres of film and television. Its storytellers, actors, and producers have built a vast audience by reflecting Nigerian life while embracing streaming platforms, international partnerships, and a confident new generation of creative talent.",
    'body' => "<p>Nollywood has grown from a resourceful home-video industry into one of the world's most energetic centres of film and television. Early filmmakers worked with limited budgets, rapid production schedules, and direct distribution networks, yet they created stories that audiences recognised immediately. Family, ambition, faith, comedy, romance, and social change were presented through a distinctly Nigerian lens.</p>

<p>That closeness to its audience became the industry's greatest strength. Films circulated across Africa and throughout the diaspora, giving viewers characters, languages, and settings that had often been absent from imported entertainment. The industry created opportunities for thousands of actors, writers, technicians, designers, musicians, and entrepreneurs while demonstrating that popular culture could also be a major source of employment.</p>

<p>Digital production and streaming have expanded Nollywood's reach and raised expectations. Larger budgets, improved training, stronger cinematography, and international co-productions now sit alongside the fast-moving independent sector. Global platforms have made Nigerian films easier to discover, while festivals and cinemas have helped ambitious productions gain critical recognition as well as commercial success.</p>

<p>Nollywood's future will depend on stronger financing, copyright protection, infrastructure, and sustainable working conditions, but its creative influence is already undeniable. It has changed how Africa sees itself on screen and how international audiences encounter Nigerian culture. Most importantly, it continues to prove that local stories, told with confidence and imagination, can travel far beyond their place of origin.</p>",
