    'body' => "<p>Since the fall of communism, Poland has undergone one of Europe's most significant political and economic transformations. The transition from a centrally planned system was often difficult, but it released entrepreneurial energy and connected Polish businesses with international markets. New institutions, private investment, and a growing services sector gradually changed the structure of the economy.</p>

<p>Membership of the European Union accelerated that process. Investment in roads, railways, cities, universities, and digital infrastructure improved connections within the country and across its borders. Polish companies became important parts of European supply chains, while workers and students gained opportunities to build experience throughout the continent and bring skills, capital, and ideas home.</p>

<p>Poland's influence also reflects its population, strategic location, and commitment to regional security. Positioned between western Europe and the EU's eastern neighbours, it has become a central voice in debates about defence, energy, enlargement, and support for democratic partners. Warsaw's decisions increasingly shape European discussions rather than simply respond to them.</p>

<p>The transformation remains unfinished, and Poland continues to debate the quality of its institutions, public services, regional inequality, and the balance between national priorities and European cooperation. Even so, the distance travelled since communism is remarkable. Poland has emerged as a resilient economy and a leading European state whose choices will help define the Union's future.</p>",
